e}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nApplying the argument of Lemma B to the new maximal sum \\(s=n\\) forces the inclusion of \\(L_{n}\\). Repeating this “peeling off” process inductively yields that every diagonal \\(L_{s}\\) for \\(s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \n\n> **[DEFENSE‑NOTE C1]** *Potential attack:* “Induction might fail because after removing a diagonal we could reuse a previously sunny line for two new points.”  \n> *Response:* At each stage the set of points with the current maximal sum contains exactly as many points as there are remaining lines. By Lemma A, any line other than the diagonal can cover at most one of those points, so the only way to meet the count is to include the diagonal itself. Thus the induction step is airtight.\n\nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) such diagonals, the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists **only** of them.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma D (No Sunny Line Can Appear)  \n\n> **Statement.** The family \\(\\mathcal F\\) described above contains **no** sunny line.\n\n**Proof.** All lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) are of the form \\(x+y=s\\); each has slope \\(-1\\) and is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\). By definition such lines are **not** sunny. ∎  \n\n> **[DEFENSE‑NOTE D1]** *Potential attack:* “The definition of sunny excludes only three directions; perhaps a line with slope \\(-1\\) but different intercept could be sunny.”  \n> *Response:* The definition says “not parallel to any of the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, **and** the line \\(x+y=0\\)”. Parallelism depends only on slope, not on intercept. Hence any line with slope \\(-1\\) is non‑sunny, regardless of its constant term.\n\n---\n\n#### Existence (Construction)  \n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{n}:=\\{\\,L_{s}: x+y=s\\mid s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny. By Lemma A each point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\) in 