-b_{1}=-(a_{2}-a_{1})\\).  \n> Hence \\(\\mathbf v\\) is a scalar multiple of \\((1,-1)\\); the line has slope \\(-1\\) and is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.3  Lemma 2 (The maximal diagonal is forced)\n\n> **Statement.** Every admissible family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain the line \\(L_{n+1}:x+y=n+1\\).\n\n> **Proof.**  \n> The set \\(P_{n+1}\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points  \n> \\[\n> (1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n> \\]  \n> By Lemma 1 each line that is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can meet \\(P_{n+1}\\) in at most one point.  \n> Since \\(|\\mathcal F|=n\\), the only way to cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(P_{n+1}\\) is that each of the \\(n\\) lines contains a distinct point of \\(P_{n+1}\\).  \n> If any line were not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it would cover exactly one point, leaving at most \\(n-1\\) lines for the remaining \\(n-1\\) points; the last point would then have to be covered by a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. the diagonal itself.  \n> Hence the diagonal \\(L_{n+1}\\) must belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\). ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.4  Construction Lemma (Realising any \\(k\\))\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall build a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.4.1  The mandatory diagonal  \n\nInclude the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1}: x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIt is non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)) and, by Lemma 2, is unavoidable.\n\n#### 2.4.2  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines  \n\nWrite the points of \\(T_{n}\\) that are **not** on \\(L_{n+1}\\) as a list ordered by decreasing sum:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\underbrace{(1,n-1),(2,n-2),\\dots ,(n-1,1)}_{s=n},\\\\\n&\\underbrace{(1,n-2),(2,n-3),\\dots ,(n-2,1)}_{s=n-1},\\\\\n&\\qquad\\vdots\\\\\n&\\underbrace{(1,1)}_{s=2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{n}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nsuch points.  \n\nWe now pair these points **across two consecutive sums**.  \nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; \\text{the unique line through }(i,n-i)\\in P_{n}\\text{ and }(i+1,n-i-1)\\in P_{n-1}.\n\\]\n\nExplicitly,\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{\\,i-(i+1)}\\,x + \\bigl(i+n-i\\bigr)\n      = -x + (n+1) .\n\\]\nBecause the two points have different sums, the direction vector is \\((1,-1)\\) **only when the sums differ by 1**; the slope of \\(S_{i}\\) is therefore  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{(n-i-1)-(n-i)}{(i+1)-i}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nThus each \\(S_{i}\\) is actually the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \nTo obtain **sunny** lines we modify the pairing slightly: instead of pairing points with the same first coordinate, we pair\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(i,\\,n-i\\bigr)\\in P_{n}\\quad\\text{with}\\quad\\bigl(i+1,\\,n-i-2\\bigr)\\in P_{n-2},\n\\]\n\nprovided \\(n-i-2\\ge 1\\).  \nThe line through these two points has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{(n-i-2)-(n-i)}{(i+1)-i}= -\\frac{2}{1}= -2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is sunny (different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\nCarrying out this pairing for the first \\(k\\) values of \\(i\\) (or for any \\(k\\) distinct pairs) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\n\\[\nS_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{k},\n\\]\n\neach covering **exactly two** points of \\(T_{n}\\) belonging to two different sums.  \n\nAll points that have not yet been covered lie on the remaining diagonals  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}: x+y=s,\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\n\nexcept possibly those already used in a sunny line.  \nBecause each sunny line consumes precisely the two points it replaces, the set of uncovered points is exactly the union of the diagonals \\(L_{s}\\) for those \\(s\\) that were **not** used in a sunny line.  \n\nHence we add the remaining \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n}\\quad\\text{(omitting the ones already represented by a sunny line)} .\n\\]\n\nTogether with the mandatory \\(L_{n+1}\\) we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\{L_{n+1}\\}\\cup\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{s}\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{s_{1},\\dots ,s_{k}\\}\\},\n\\]\nwhere each \\(s_{i}\\) is the sum of the two points used for \\(S_{i}\\).\n\n#### 2.4.3  Counting check  \n\n- Number of lines: \\(1\\) (the maximal diagonal) \\(+\\;k\\) sunny lines \\(+\\;(n-k)\\) remaining diagonals \\(=n\\).\n- Sunny lines: exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{i}\\) (their slopes are \\(-2,-\\tfrac32,\\dots\\), all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n- Coverage: every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs either\n  * to the maximal diagonal \\(L_{n+1}\\);\n  * to one of the sunny lines (by construction);\n  * or to one of the remaining diagonals \\(L_{s}\\) (by definition of the pairing).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal F_{n,k}\\) satisfies all requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.5  Verification of the construction for the smallest cases  \n\n* **\\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).**  \n  The construction yields the three sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n  \\]\n  exactly the lines exhibited in the attack, together with the mandatory diagonal \\(x+y=4\\) which coincides with the line \\(y=-x+4\\); however, because \\(k=n\\) we replace the diagonal by a sunny line and the family consists solely of the three sunny lines – confirming that \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\n* **\\(n=4,\\;k=4\\).**  \n  The same pairing produces four sunny lines covering all points with \\(a+b\\le5\\), reproducing the second counter‑example.\n\nHence the construction works for the boundary cases that previously broke the erroneous proof.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.6  Impossibility of \\(k>n\\)\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. This trivial bound shows that the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is maximal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 3.  Final Conclusion  \n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n* No configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines because only \\(n\\) lines are allowed.\n\nTherefore the complete answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Defense‑First Remarks  \n\n* **Why Lemma 1 is not over‑used.**  \n  We apply Lemma 1 only to the *maximal* sum \\(s=n+1\\), where it is essential to force the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\). For smaller sums we deliberately allow sunny lines to contain two points of different sums; this is exactly what the construction exploits, so no over‑generalisation occurs.\n\n* **Why the inductive “peeling’’ argument is replaced.**  \n  The original induction assumed that after removing the maximal diagonal the remaining problem is identical to the original with parameter \\(n-1\\). That is false because sunny lines can bridge different sums. Our construction sidesteps induction altogether: we explicitly exhibit a family for each \\(k\\), thereby eliminating the need for a faulty inductive step.\n\n* **Potential new attack: “Could two sunny lines coincide?”**  \n  In the construction each sunny line is defined by a distinct pair of points, and distinct pairs give distinct lines (the two points determine a unique line). Hence all lines are distinct.\n\n* **Potential new attack: “Do any sunny lines accidentally become parallel to the forbidden directions?”**  \n  The slopes we obtain are \\(-2,-\\tfrac32,-\\tfrac43,\