at most \\(n\\) points in total.  \n   The whole set \\(T_{n}:=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}P_{s}\\) has  \n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n   points. Since one line (the forced diagonal) already covers the \\(n\\) points of \\(P_{n+1}\\), the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the other\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n   \\]\n   points.\n\n4. **Parity obstruction.**  \n   Every sunny line is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, hence it can meet each diagonal in at most one point (Lemma 1).  \n   A non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); such a line also meets each diagonal in at most one point.  \n   Therefore **every** line, sunny or not, contributes at most one point to each diagonal.  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.  \n   On a fixed diagonal \\(P_{s}\\) the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).  \n   Since \\(|P_{s}|=s-1\\), the only diagonals that can be *fully* covered are those with \\(s-1\\le n\\), i.e. all of them.  \n   However, the **parity** of the number of points on a diagonal matters: the points of \\(P_{s}\\) can be split into pairs \\(\\{(a,b),(b,a)\\}\\) except when \\(s\\) is even, in which case there is a single “central’’ point \\((\\tfrac{s}{2},\\tfrac{s}{2})\\).  \n   A sunny line cannot pass through a central point of an even‑sum diagonal together with any other point of the same diagonal (Lemma 1), so each such central point must be covered by a **distinct** line.  \n   There are exactly \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) even‑sum diagonals among \\(P_{2},\\dots ,P_{n}\\). Hence at least \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) lines are forced to be *non‑sunny* in order to hit those central points.  \n\n   Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n   \\[\n   k\\le n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor = \\lceil n/2\\rceil .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction achie