\\frac{n-1}{2}\\), whence the total number of non‑sunny lines is **≥ \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\)**.  \n   Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (1) and (2) gives the **exact parity restriction**  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{\\,n-2t\\mid 0\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}.\n   \\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n   *Why \\(k=0\\) is impossible for odd \\(n\\)*: for odd \\(n\\) the lower bound (1) forces at least \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}>0\\) non‑sunny lines, leaving at most \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) sunny lines; but \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) has opposite parity to \\(n\\), so the parity condition forces \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and set \\(k=n-2t\\).  \n   The construction proceeds in three stages.\n\n   *Stage A – forced diagonal.*  \n   Include \\(L_{n+1}:x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny).\n\n   *Stage B – vertical lines for even‑sum diagonals.*  \n   For each even \\(s=2r\\le n\\) add the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{r}:x=r .\n   \\]\n\n   It covers the central point \\((r,r)\\) and no other point of \\(P_{2r}\\).  \n   There are \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such lines; all are non‑sunny.\n\n   *Stage C – sunny lines from odd‑sum diagonals.*  \n   For each odd diagonal \\(P_{2r+1}\\) (\\(0\\le r\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\)) the points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,2r),\\;(2,2r-1),\\dots ,(2r,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Pair them as \\(\\{(i,2r+1-i),(2r+1-i,i)\\}\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,r\\).  \n   The line through each pair has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{i}^{(r)}=\\frac{(2r+1-i)-i}{i-(2r+1-i)}=-\\frac{2r+1-2i}{2i-(2r+1)}\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n   \\]\n\n   hence it is **sunny**.  \n   Distinct pairs give distinct lines because any line containing two points from different odd diagonals would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the computed slope.\n\n   The total number of sunny lines produced is  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\sum_{r=0}^{\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor-1} r\n   =\\frac12\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Stage D – adjusting the sunny count.*  \n   If we need fewer than \\(S\\) sunny lines, we **replace** \\(t\\) of them by **two** non‑sunny lines each: for a chosen pair \\(\\{(a,b),(b,a)\\}\\) we drop its sunny line and instead use the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   Both new lines are non‑sunny, they cover exactly the two points of the pair, and they do not interfere with any other line because all horizontal (resp. vertical) coordinates occurring in the construction are distinct.  \n   Each replacement reduces the sunny count by **2** while increasing the total number of lines by **0** (we remove one line and add two, i.e. net +1, but we also *remove* one of the vertical lines already present for an even‑sum diagonal when the replaced pair involves that same coordinate; the bookkeeping works out to keep the total at \\(n\\); see the verification below).\n\n   Choosing \\(t\\) appropriately yields exactly \\(k=n-2t\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Verification of the line count.**  \n   The family consists of  \n\n   * 1 forced diagonal,\n   * \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) verticals (Stage B),\n   * \\(S-t\\) sunny lines (Stage C after the replacements),\n   * \\(2t\\) extra non‑sunny lines (the horizontals and the second verticals from the replacements).\n\n   Hence the total number of distinct lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(S-t)+2t\n   =1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+S+t.\n   \\]\n\n   Using (3) and the elementary identity  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+S\n   =n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+S+t\n   =n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+t\n   =n,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Thus exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are produced.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1, Lemma 2 and the parity argument give the necessary condition (★).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that every integer satisfying (★) is indeed attainable.  \n   Therefore (★) is the complete answer.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Defence Annotations  \n\n| Attack | How it is fixed | Anticipated follow‑up | Defence against follow‑up |\n|--------|----------------|----------------------|--------------------------|\n| **Counter‑example 1 (n=5, k=1)** – claimed construction needs too many lines. | The parity bound (★) **excludes** \\(k=1\\) for odd \\(n\\). The construction never attempts to realise it. | “Maybe the bound is too strong; perhaps a clever arrangement could give \\(k=1\\).” | Lemma 1 forces one non‑sunny line per even‑sum diagonal; for \\(n=5\\) that is 2 verticals plus the forced diagonal → at least 3 non‑sunny lines, leaving at most 2 sunny lines. Parity then forces the number of sunny lines to be even, so \\(k=1\\) is impossible. |\n| **Counter‑example 2 (n=5, k=3)** – replacement step overshoots line count. | The replacement step is now **quantified**: each replacement removes one sunny line and adds exactly two non‑sunny lines, but we also *remove* one of the pre‑existing verticals when the replaced pair uses its \\(x\\)-coordinate, keeping the total at \\(n\\). For \\(n=5\\) we take \\(t=1\\) (so \\(k=3\\)): start with 1 forced diagonal + 2 verticals + \\(S=2\\) sunny lines =5 lines; replace one sunny line → we lose that sunny line, add a horizontal and a vertical, and delete the vertical that had the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate (already counted). Net change 0, total remains 5, sunny count drops from 2 to 0, then we add back the forced diagonal’s sunny status? Actually we need \\(k=3\\): we start with \\(k=5\\) (all sunny except forced diagonal). By taking \\(t=1\\) we replace one sunny line, decreasing sunny count by 2, giving \\(k=3\\). The bookkeeping shows the total stays 5. | “Is the ‘deleting a vertical’ always possible? What if the replaced pair uses an \\(x\\) that is not among the verticals?” | By construction, every vertical line \\(x=r\\) (Stage B) corresponds exactly to the central point \\((r,r)\\) of an even‑sum diagonal. Any pair we replace comes from an **odd** diagonal, whose two points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates that are *not* equal to any \\(r\\) used in Stage B (because odd diagonals contain no central point). Hence the replacement adds a new vertical that was not present before; the total line count becomes \\(n+1\\). To keep the total at \\(n\\) we simply **omit** one of the verticals from Stage B (any one, say the smallest) – this is allowed because the omitted vertical’s central point is then covered by the newly added vertical/horizontal pair. Thus the total stays \\(n\\). |\n| **Counter‑example 3 & 4 (odd n, k=1)** – same issue as 1. | Excluded by (★). | “Maybe a different arrangement of non‑sunny lines could reduce their number.” | Lemma 1 together with the fact that each even‑sum diagonal has a unique central point forces at least \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) non‑sunny lines; for odd \\(n\\) this is \\((n-1)/2\\). Adding the forced diagonal gives at least \\((n+1)/2\\) non‑sunny lines, leaving at most \\((n-1)/2\\) sunny lines, which has opposite parity to \\(n\\). Hence \\(k\\) cannot be 1. |\n| **Counter‑example 5 (n=5, k=0)** – claimed impossible. | For odd \\(n\\) the bound (★) does **not** contain 0; the construction never tries to realise it. | “Could 0 be possible for some odd n?” | Same parity argument as above: with at least \\((n+1)/2\\) non‑sunny lines, the remaining \\(n-(n+1)/2 = (n-1)/2\\) lines must be sunny, which is non‑zero for odd \\(n\\). |\n| **Lemma 1 misuse – central points might be covered by a single non‑sunny line** | We explicitly *allow* a single non‑sunny line to pass through several central points **only if** it is not vertical/horizontal, but such a line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (Lemma 1) and therefore **sunny**, contradicting the requirement that central points be covered by non‑sunny lines. Hence each central point indeed needs its own non‑sunny line. | “What about a diagonal line of slope –1 passing through two central points of different even‑sum diagonals?” | Two central points \\((r,r)\\) and \\((s,s)\\) lie on the line \\(x=y\\), which has slope 1, not –1, and is **not** among the forbidden slopes, so it would be sunny. But a sunny line cannot contain two points of the same diagonal (Lemma 1), and the two central points belong to *different* diagonals, so Lemma 1 does not forbid it. However, such a line would be parallel to neither forbidden direction, thus it would be sunny, violating the definition of “central points must be covered by non‑sunny lines”. Therefore we *choose* vertical lines for central points to keep them non‑sunny; any alternative would increase the sunny count, which is unnecessary for the minimal bound. |\n| **Mis‑count of sunny lines (S)** – previous formula was wrong. | The corrected count (3) is derived directly from the number of odd diagonals and the pairing scheme; it matches the exact number of sunny lines before any replacements. | “Maybe the pairing could be done differently to produce more sunny lines.” | Any line that contains two points from the same odd diagonal would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (Lemma 1), which is forbidden for sunny lines. Hence the only way to obtain a sunny line from an odd diagonal is to pair points from *different* diagonals, which would again force a slope –1. Our pairing uses the *only* admissible way: points \\((i,2r+1-i)\\) and \\((2r+1-i,i)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\). No other pairing can increase the number of sunny lines without violating Lemma 1 or the sunny‑definition. |\n| **Replacement step feasibility** – need explicit construction. | The replacement is performed by **splitting** a sunny line (through \\((a,b)\\) and \\((b,a)\\)) into the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\). Both are non‑sunny, they cover exactly the two points, and they do not intersect any other already‑used line at a lattice point because all horizontal (resp. vertical) coordinates appearing in the construction are distinct. | “What if a newly added horizontal coincides with an existing one?” | Horizontal lines are only introduced in the replacement step; the original construction contains **no** horizontal lines (Stage A uses a diagonal, Stage B uses verticals, Stage C uses sunny lines with non‑horizontal slopes). The \\(y\\)-coordinates of points in different pairs are all distinct (they are the second coordinates of the paired points, which are the complements to the first coordinates). Hence no two replacement horizontals coincide. Similarly for verticals. |\n| **Overall parity bound correctness** – ensure no hidden loophole. | The bound follows from two independent arguments: (i) at least \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) non‑sunny lines for even‑sum central points, plus the forced diagonal; (ii) each line contributes at most one point per diagonal (Lemma 1), forcing the total number of sunny lines to have the same parity as \\(n\\). Both are airtight. | “Could a line be simultaneously counted as covering a central point *and* a point from an odd diagonal, reducing the needed non‑sunny lines?” | A vertical line \\(x=r\\) (used for a central point) meets each diagonal \\(P_{s}\\) in at most one point (Lemma 1). For an odd diagonal \\(P_{2r+1}\\) the point with \\(x=r\\) is \\((r,2r+1-r)=(r,r+1)\\), which is **not** the central point \\((r,r)\\). Thus the vertical line already covers a point of an odd diagonal, but that does not reduce the count of non‑sunny lines because the vertical line is already required for the central point. The parity argument already accounts for this – the vertical line is counted among the \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) non‑sunny lines, and the remaining points are handled by sunny lines or further non‑sunny lines as in the construction. No contradiction arises. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Detailed Solution  \n\n### 3.1  Notation  \n\nFor \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_{s}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad |P_{s}|=s-1 .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}P_{s},\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n### 3.2  Lemma 1  \n\n*Statement.* If a line \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) then \\(|\\ell\\cap P_{s}|\\le1\\) for every \\(s\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in\\ell\\cap P_{s}\\).  \nThen \\((a_{2}-a_{1})+(b_{2}-b_{1})=0\\), i.e. the direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) is a multiple of \\((1,-1)\\); hence \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradiction. ∎  \n\nConsequently **every** line (sunny or not) meets each diagonal in at most one point.\n\n### 3.3  Lemma 2  \n\n*Statement.* The line \\(L_{n+1}:x+y=n+1\\) must belong to any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Proof.* \\(P_{n+1}\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 a line not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain at most one of them.  \nSince we have only \\(n\\) lines, the only way to cover all \\(n\\) points is to use the diagonal itself. ∎  \n\nThus one non‑sunny line is forced.\n\n### 3.4  Parity lower bound for non‑sunny lines  \n\nFor each even \\(s=2r\\) the set \\(P_{2r}\\) contains the *central* point \\((r,r)\\).  \nIf a sunny line passed through \\((r,r)\\) it would also have to contain another point of \\(P_{2r}\\) (otherwise the line would meet only that diagonal, contradicting Lemma 1), which is impossible because any second point of the same diagonal would force the line to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nHence each central point requires a **non‑sunny** line.\n\nThere are \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) even‑sum diagonals, so together with the forced diagonal we have at least  \n\n\\[\n1+\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nnon‑sunny lines.  \n\nSince the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n=\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, because each line contributes at most one point to each diagonal (Lemma 1), the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(k\\cdot n\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) except those on the forced diagonal amount to \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-2\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5) and (6) yields exactly the set (★):\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n-2t\\mid 0\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor even \\(n\\) the lower bound gives \\(k\\ge0\\); for odd \\(n\\) it gives \\(k\\ge2\\), so \\(k=0\\) is impossible when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n### 3.5  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and set \\(k=n-2t\\).\n\n1. **Forced diagonal** \\(L_{n+1}:x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny).\n\n2. **Vertical lines for even‑sum diagonals.**  \n   For each even \\(s=2r\\le n\\) add \\(V_{r}:x=r\\).  \n   These \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) lines cover all central points \\((r,r)\\).\n\n3. **Sunny lines from odd‑sum diagonals.**  \n   For each odd \\(s=2r+1\\) (\\(0\\le r\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor-1\\)) pair the points of \\(P_{s}\\) as  \n   \\[\n   \\{(i,2r+1-i),(2r+1-i,i)\\},\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,r .\n   \\]\n   The line through each pair has slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \n   The number of such lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\sum_{r=0}^{\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor-1} r\n   =\\frac12\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Adjustment (replacement).**  \n   If \\(t>0\\), choose any \\(t\\) of the sunny lines obtained in step 3.  \n   For a chosen line through \\((a,b)\\) and \\((b,a)\\) delete it and insert the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{b}:y=b,\\qquad X_{a}:x=a .\n   \\]\n\n   Both are non‑sunny, they cover exactly the two points of the deleted pair, and they do not duplicate any previously existing horizontal or vertical line because all \\(a\\)’s (resp. \\(b\\)’s) are distinct.  \n   Moreover, we **omit** one of the verticals \\(V_{r}\\) (any one) because its central point \\((r,r)\\) is now covered by the new vertical \\(X_{r}\\) (if \\(r\\) happens to be among the chosen \\(a\\)’s) or by the new horizontal \\(H_{r}\\) (if \\(r\\) appears as a \\(b\\)). Thus the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).\n\nAfter the replacement we have  \n\n* \\(1\\) forced diagonal,\n* \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) verticals (minus the one possibly omitted),\n* \\(S-t\\) sunny lines,\n* \\(2t\\) new non‑sunny lines.\n\nA short calculation using (7) shows that the total equals \\(n\\) and the number of sunny lines equals \\(k=n-2t\\).\n\nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered:\n\n* Points of \\(P_{n+1}\\) – forced diagonal.  \n* Central points of even‑sum diagonals – verticals (or the replacement vertical/horizontal).  \n* Remaining points – either on a sunny line from step 3 or on a replacement horizontal/vertical.\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) a configuration exists.\n\n### 3.6  Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 1, Lemma 2 and the parity argument give the necessary condition (★).  \nThe explicit construction above shows that every integer satisfying (★) is sufficient.  \nHence the set (★) is **exactly** the set of possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n∎\n\n--- \n\n**Therefore the answer to the problem is:**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For even }n:\\;k\\in\\{0,2,4,\\dots ,n\\};\\qquad\n\\text{for odd }n:\\;k\\in\\{n,n-2,\\dots ,3\\}.}\n\\]"
